    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Atopic dermatitis (AD) disrupts epidermal keratinocytes and causes an impaired structural and immune skin barrier. This results in an environment favored by the bacteria Staphylococcus aureus (S.a), which colonizes the skin of 90% of AD patients. While colonization with S.a. exacerbates the disease, the molecular mechanisms underlying AD are not completely understood. We have previously found that keratinocyte-specific deletion of the gene encoding RAB guanine nucleotide exchange factor 1 (RABGEF-1) activates the MyD88 pathway and severely impairs epidermal barrier function in mice. This induces many features of the AD phenotype. Deletion of RABGEF-1 in keratinocytes also activates the MyD88 signaling pathway, which subsequently activates transcription of inflammatory cytokines and thus contributes to the disrupted skin homeostasis. Furthermore, the Galli lab has found that the increased presence of commensal S.a., as well as clinically relevant toxin-positive and toxin-negative S.a. strains, can exacerbate the AD-like skin condition in RABGEF-1 deficient mice. We therefore hypothesized that only the cell wall of gram-positive heat-killed (HK) S.a. is sufficient to exacerbate AD-like pathology, as peptidoglycan and lipoteichoic acid—major components of such cell walls—can stimulate Toll-like Receptor 2 (TLR2), which can subsequently activate MyD88. Using a cre-inducible mouse model, we applied tamoxifen to excise the RABGEF-1 gene and induce an AD-like skin condition. We then applied heat killed (HK) S.a., which did not substantially exacerbate AD-related skin pathology in mice whose keratinocytes were depleted of RABGEF-1. However, it did increase their weight loss when compared to the corresponding mice treated with water or LPS. This revealed that HK S.a., whether acting on TLR2 or other pathogen recognition receptors, is not sufficient to exacerbate the features of AD-like skin pathology in mice whose keratinocytes are depleted of RABGEF-1; yet, HK S.a. may increase systemic effects of RABGEF-1-mediated skin disease.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
